@ 47.1 - List 1: Simple Consonant Changes
	w > p (Navajo, some Polynesian languages)
	kʲ ɡʲ > k ɡ (P:[+voice]anish)
	ś > k (Possibly unconditional; some Samoyedic langs)
	pʲ > kʲ (some Somanian dialect͡s, P:[-voice]sakonian)
	t͡s > t (unconditional; some Samoyedic langs)
	t > k (general Polynesian)
	n > ŋ (Samoan, but only in colloquial speech)
	j > p (some Polynesian languages, such as Levei and P:[+voice]rehet) (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proto-:[+affricate]ustronesian_language)
	b > #c, -nc- (Sundanese)
	ŋ > {x,h} (various Mayan languages)
	h > ŋ (Nyole)
	ʕ > ŋ (allegedly in:[+front]uropean:[+laryngeal]ebrew, both Sephardic and:[+affricate]shkenazi, but possibly not a sound change so much as a substitution) (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/[+laryngeal]ebrew_language#Varieties_of_ayin, http://sites.google.com/site/londonsephardiminhag/pronunciation, http://www.forward.com/articles/105938/)
	f > p (? claimed to have occurred independently in Proto-Semitic and Proto-Omotic, and to a limited extent in:[+front]gyptian (but this may be related to the Semitic change); note that the:[+approximant]ikipedia article is cited to a single source and that source is admittedly theoretical, and acknowledges on page 77 another reconstruction that doesn’t believe Proto-:[+affricate]fro-:[+affricate]siatic had /f/ at all) (Page 77 of http://is.gd/[+approximant]NyXdn, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proto-:[+affricate]fro-:[+affricate]siatic_language)
	tš > t (general:[+back]altic-Finnic; may not be unconditional but was certainly the most common outcome)
	r > ɡʟ
	ɡʟ > ɣ ([+laryngeal]iw) (http://is.gd/jCP:[+voice]LO1)
	mb nd > ʙ dr (Nias)